# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CREDIFY ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the real values:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is listed in .gitignore and must never be committed. This file, with empty
# values, IS committed, so anyone cloning the repository can see what is needed.
#
# Note that NEITHER name below starts with NEXT_PUBLIC_. That prefix is what tells Next.js
# to inline a value into the JavaScript it sends to the browser. Without it, these values
# stay on the server and are unreachable from the client, which is exactly what we want for
# a database key.
#
# There is deliberately NO publishable key here. The browser never talks to Supabase in
# this MVP, so it has no need of one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
# Looks like: https://abcdefghijklmnop.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=

# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys -> "Publishable and secret API keys"
# -> create or reveal a SECRET key. It starts with `sb_secret_`.
#
# This is the current replacement for the legacy `service_role` key, which Supabase is
# deprecating. An old `service_role` JWT (a long `eyJ...` string) still works here if you
# have one, but new projects should use a secret key.
#
# THIS KEY BYPASSES ALL DATABASE SECURITY RULES (Postgres BYPASSRLS). Treat it like a
# database password:
#   - never commit it
#   - never paste it into client-side code, a browser console, or a chat window
#   - if it is ever exposed, revoke it in the Supabase dashboard immediately
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=
